    "title": "TSA lifts shoe removal rule nationwide - NewsBreak",
    "text": "U.S. airports will no longer require passengers to remove their shoes during preflight security checks, Department of Homeland Security Secretary Kristi Noem announced Tuesday. Why it matters: The shoes-off rule, imposed in 2006 after a Miami-bound passenger snuck explosives in his shoe on a flight from Paris, is an infamous source of stress for travelers. Driving the news: Transportation Security Agency checkpoints at airports across the country will begin imposing the new policy immediately, Noem said at a press conference Tuesday. What they're saying: \"We've evaluated the equipment that every airport has and are fully confident in the no-shoes policy that we still have the security [capabilities] in place at every single checkpoint.\" Frequent flyers who pay for TSA PreCheck and undergo a screening process could already keep their shoes on. Editor's note: This story has been updated to reflect that the shoe-removal policy's end, which was initially reported as being implemented at only some airports, is now being rolled out nationwide.",
